  "body": "## What was wrong\n\nThe Get Started checklist shipped in 4.29.0 told you *what* to do but not *how*, and its button dropped you at the top of a long settings page with no indication of which control it meant. \"Connect a model provider\" → Providers, then hunt.\n\n## What changed\n\n**Per-step instructions.** Each step carries an ordered list of concrete steps naming the real controls. Every label cited was checked against `index.html` rather than written from memory — the footer button is literally `Apply`, the nav entries are `Providers` and `Model Config`. A wrong label in a walkthrough costs more than a wrong label anywhere else: the reader looks for the thing you named and concludes the app is broken when it simply is not there.\n\n**Buttons that point at the right place.** Each step carries an optional DOM target, so the button scrolls to and briefly highlights the specific card or field. Every selector was confirmed present before being wired.\n\n**It reads as a walkthrough, not a wall.** The first incomplete required step is expanded with its instructions visible; everything else collapses to one line. It also *advances* — once the expanded step becomes done, the next incomplete required step takes its place.\n\nThat last part was the first version's real defect, caught in review: the expanded id was chosen once and never re-derived, so completing the provider step left it expanded showing \"Done\" while the next action sat collapsed out of sight — defeating the entire premise of the feature.\n\n## Nothing new is persisted\n\nWhich step is expanded is view state. The only two things this feature stores remain `dismissed` and the guide's `visited` list; every done-state is still derived from live configuration (Part IV §5).\n\n## Verification\n\nAgainst a live server on a throwaway `HOME`:\n\n```\nGET /admin/api/onboarding\nrequired 0/3  complete=False\n  provider    done=False  target='#providerGrid'                    instructions=4\n  models      done=False  target='[data-key=\"MODEL\"]'               instructions=3\n  client      done=False  target='#claudeSettingsPanel'             instructions=3\n  websearch   done=True   target='[data-key=\"WEB_SEARCH_PROVIDER\"]' instructions=4\n  messaging   done=False  target='[data-key=\"MESSAGING_PLATFORM\"]'  instructions=4\n  analytics   done=False  target='#reqStatsCards'                   instructions=3\n  guide       done=False  target=None                               instructions=1\n```\n\nA brand-new install reporting **0 of 3** is the assertion worth having. The one step showing done is web search, because a real `EXA_API_KEY` is present in this shell and `load_value_state` reads `os.environ` — the same known local artifact behind 8 of the 14 baseline test failures, not a defect.\n\n```\n$ uv run ty check\nAll checks passed!\n\n$ env -u ENABLE_WEB_SERVER_TOOLS -u EXA_API_KEY uv run pytest tests/config tests/api tests/contracts -q\n1061 passed in 42.38s\n```\n\n`node --check` clean on `admin.js`. Rebased onto `ae7c7e1` (4.31.0) and re-verified there, since that release also changed `admin.js`.\n\n## Residual risks\n\n- **The collapse/expand interaction and the scroll-and-highlight have not been driven in a browser.** The state machine is covered by reading it and the API payload above is real, but the highlight itself is unverified.\n- Instructions are static prose. If a control is renamed later, nothing asserts these strings still match the UI — the same unguarded docs-drift already noted for `docs/USAGE.md` and the in-dashboard Guide.\n- The messaging step still knows Telegram and Discord by name rather than deriving from a catalog, unchanged from 4.29.0.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
